the federal reserve has been absolutely  under fire when it comes to the  controversy of fed presidents and other  officials that have high ranking  positions  trading sometimes even day trading  various assets that either the fed is  investing in or that they are getting  rich off of while there are many many  americans that are actually not doing so  hot because of the fed's activities we  now have  a semi solution federal reserve is  implementing new rules that will stop  senior officials from trading individual  stocks or bonds you want to work at the  fed you can't trade individual stocks or  bonds you can still invest in index  funds and mutual funds and those types  of assets but you can't actually own or  trade them now the officials will be  restricted primarily to owning mutual  funds which they will have to hold for a  year  and they will need permission to buy or  sell them so again this is a direct  response to the fact that we found out  that multiple fed officials were  literally trading on a frequent basis at  the same time and in some cases buying  and selling the same assets that the fed  was buying and selling they have inside  information now it is not true insider  trading because ultimately it is not  material non-public information from a  corporation but it's almost even more  valuable if you know what the central  bank is going to do before everyone else  and so we've got two quotes here the  first coming from jerome powell he says  these tough new rules raise the bar high  in order to assure the public we serve  that all of our senior officials  maintain a single-minded focus on the  public mission of the federal reserve we  got a second quote from  george selgen who says it's probably a  wise move because the fact is that  distinguishing between genuine insider  trading and just ordinary trades that  look like they might be taking advantage  of insider information is fraught with  problems now with this i actually will  give the federal reserve a lot of credit  here one there was a controversy they  pretty quickly acted in terms of  accepting the resignations i'm not going  to say they pushed anybody out but some  of the people who were embroiled in this  controversy they no longer are at the  federal reserve the second thing they  did is they said we are going to look  take a look at what has occurred and  also what our current policies are and  now just within a couple of weeks they  come out with brand new guidance for  those senior officials new rules that  they must be subjected to including that  they cannot own or trade individual  stocks and bonds that's a pretty good  step but then two is that you have to  actually buy mutual funds you have to  hold it for a year and you need  permission to buy or sell them  so what this looks like to me is the  fact that we actually have a pretty  tough set of rules that is going to be  implemented against the federal  officials the last thing that i will say  is if it's good enough for the fed it  should be good enough for all of the  politicians as well we should take this  exact framework that everyone is  celebrating that now we have appointed  officials who are going to actually not  be allowed to own individual stocks and  bonds that they will have to hold them  for an entire year if it's in a mutual  fund and they need permission to buy and  sell  so should every single politician if  you're a senator or you're a congressman  we don't need you day trading we do not  need you speculating on financial  markets we actually don't even need you  buying and selling individual stocks  that you may actually have the ability  to oversee instead what you should do is  you should be forced to sell all of your  individual stocks you can own indexes or  mutual funds you should have to hold  them for at least one year and you  should have to ask for permission to buy  or sell them the exact same framework  that the federal reserve is implementing  here sounds pretty good we should give a  kudos so congratulations to the federal  reserve for acting not only swiftly but  also with the severity in terms of what  those new rules are going to be but we  should then learn from our mistakes and  we should extrapolate those exact rules  and we should apply them across the  political spectrum every single  congressman and senator should not be  allowed to trade and profit off of  individual stocks and bonds while they  are in office you want to do it before  you're in office no problem you do when  you leave office no problem but while  you serve in office you should not be  allowed to actually do this my guess  though is i'm not going to hold my  breath i do not think that either side  of the aisle is going to agree to this  and as we have seen with many many  pieces of legislation in the past there  is usually this idea of do what i say  not what i do and so ultimately what i  think we're going to see is a lot of  congratulations a lot of applause a lot  of padding on the back federal reserve  great job coming from politicians but  they will not implement this rules for  themselves there is just too much money  the economic and financial incentive is  too high for their ability to trade in  and out of individual stocks and bonds  when  the opposite would simply be owning  mutual funds
 

